#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221188 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "99f2f8a2-f5aa-5a32-b29f-8e1f79f504cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21692" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/cd/cd022d759a5ce5a8521687d42eaf38053339212301a6b3559ad4bee935f69317.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f5/f51930ce883e8e2f4865d846b38c475e68875565593ad58044125e81f0443718.svg)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/53/53cec1a04bca84fba279c48cf5a531674040c07e2a1dac12748107b91809ff72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/05/05e9d31a8413096e60f8d30b8206052375dc7c9df5895568a33b475fca22fc72.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221189 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e61881ec-4361-554d-b374-35b8b3f64366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21693" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/33/33beedce1869f8f3a894434acc7c0a89a94062470e7dacd8a5e14a2d61ef36b5.svg) and ![](images/ac/ac4b467f8441d1473393728f7e9a05fd36b106044e73c151780b963db77bdbfa.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/7b/7b764933668ae61643bf324c8a1275df1f5c2ca778153103e687f2ce77719c5d.svg)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221190 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/id #uuid "9215f93a-14ad-5be5-9f27-25ba556252e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21694" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\nI. $y=3x+3$\n\nII. $y=-x$\n\nIII. $y=3x$\n\nIV. $y=-3x$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b548975-82b4-57b7-b2e8-0a87a8cc8fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)+3 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431867} {:answer/id #uuid "67ce320b-106e-566c-80ef-6487f5a73923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431870} {:answer/id #uuid "85ac10bf-7f54-5f23-9b48-972bdcaa5d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431873} {:answer/id #uuid "95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431876} {:answer/id #uuid "bf509ea2-55f9-56fd-8f16-318399695d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431876} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e127c2-d85f-50d8-8cca-75bad3e7ba96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bff1e770-1e9c-53a8-97b5-2a7a2fa56278" :question/math-academy-id "q-333664" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f38395a-e0c9-5a4c-9a38-9a34b59867d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431865} {:answer/id #uuid "24616f82-f759-536a-a547-f8851f776bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431868} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04fe5c-6c47-5e1c-9d6c-94fd18017163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431872} {:answer/id #uuid "c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431881} {:answer/id #uuid "ff527e63-d538-51a6-b3c5-fcaa3897bb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431881} :answer-field/id #uuid "819f1de9-2d6b-59f9-9ce0-5429b3d56920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb1b21bd-521d-5db5-99c4-385125d3ec35" :question/math-academy-id "q-335196" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192d2edf-e2b5-5feb-98f3-9b9f0bb71069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-3{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431866} {:answer/id #uuid "92c98d09-569a-504a-8066-e5395a7b1b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431875} {:answer/id #uuid "b901670b-07a9-55c2-a7e8-31763938cbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4{u}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431878} {:answer/id #uuid "c461459c-bd05-5ec8-a414-0b65c4b944a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431880} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431882} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f03df0-1c1a-500e-83f5-21bfe8839deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13f56d3d-b69f-5f92-a641-c6c370a1ed21" :question/math-academy-id "q-335383" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+2y(t)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+2,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+2)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+5(v(t)+1)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-{v}^{2}(t)-2v(t)-1+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+2y(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+2)^{2}-4(u(t)+2)+2(v(t)+1)+2 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4)-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+2+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+4 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431869} {:answer/id #uuid "6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431871} {:answer/id #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431874} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431877} {:answer/id #uuid "f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431874} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" :question/math-academy-id "q-335386" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,3]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "334536ed-4953-5436-88cc-60f156831174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309138} {:answer/id #uuid "63bfd85f-d094-5a8c-a47c-6d4e944d4da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{w}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309514} {:answer/id #uuid "8752cf48-cece-54d1-ac9b-3ef5f58fe9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{w}_{2}-{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309811} {:answer/id #uuid "88e4ff15-3067-5370-87da-bb446df2da3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309825} {:answer/id #uuid "c70fc932-5bb2-5038-95e6-5b2bb2bf51d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{w}_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63bfd85f-d094-5a8c-a47c-6d4e944d4da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{w}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309514} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aace687-46d0-5cf1-b520-b8e36d240124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b72c3d1f-8eb6-5972-9e5b-a9b662d68cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335484" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\,3]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Bu.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $B?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $B$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${w}_{1}$ or ${w}_{2}$ is the vector $5{w}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f955582-6bf8-5f7e-95a5-2f31b775517c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309468} {:answer/id #uuid "78d0b2b9-f2f7-54b2-954f-a3089a1cc610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An asymptotically stable spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309695} {:answer/id #uuid "b9682602-16ab-56f2-8f54-1a54dcdfd505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An asymptotically stable sink (stable node)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310254} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4f2f54-0a0c-5cf3-8f94-d0a32b9fc21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stable center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310404} {:answer/id #uuid "f7942cd2-9621-5bb0-977d-04edfae5f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7942cd2-9621-5bb0-977d-04edfae5f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca54eeb2-16ef-5ec6-a4c2-4091eade95ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05ade684-7cd1-5147-822c-7acb8eafb6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-335515" :question/problem "A system of ODEs has an unstable saddle point at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"An unstable saddle point\"."}]}
